{"version":"2026.10.11-r25","id":"Q413314","formula":"CuPbSbS₃","hill":"CuPbS3Sb","elements":["S","Cu","Sb","Pb"],"composition":{"S":3.0,"Cu":1.0,"Pb":1.0,"Sb":1.0},"names":{"en":"Bournonite","ru":"Бурнонит","uk":"Бурноніт"},"descriptions":{"en":"Bournonite, also axotomous antimony glance, wheel ore, berthonite, volchite or dystomic glance (German: antimonbleikupferblende) is a sulfosalt mineral species, trithioantimoniate of lead and copper with the formula PbCuSbS3. It was first mentioned by Philip Rashleigh in 1797 as an ore of antimony and was more completely described in 1804 by French crystallographer and mineralogist Jacques Louis, Comte de Bournon (1751–1825), after whom it was named. The name given by Bournon himself (in 1813) was endellione, since used in the form endellionite, after St Endellion, the locality in Cornwall where the mineral was first found.","ru":"Бурнони́т (англ. Bournonite), также бертонит или аксотомический сурьмяный блеск (устар.) — минерал подкласса сульфосолей (сложных сульфидов), химическая формула CuPbSbS3. Содержание: Рb — 42,5%, Cu — 13%, Sb — 24,7%, S — 19,8%; часты примеси железа (до 5 %), серебра (до 3 %), Zn, Mn, Ni, Bi, As.","uk":"Бурноніт — мінерал класу сульфосолей."},"fame":31,"pubchemCid":null,"mass":null,"charge":0,"smiles":null,"inchikey":null,"iupac":null,"classes":[{"qid":"Q12089225","names":{"en":"mineral species","ru":"действительный вид минералов","uk":"мінеральний вид"}}],"structureInfo":{"kind":"crystal","source":"materialsproject","ref":"mp-649774","atoms":30,"license":"CC BY 4.0, Materials Project"},"structure":{"cell":[[7.885,0.0,0.0],[0.0,8.287,0.0],[0.0,0.0,8.816]],"kind":"crystal","atoms":[{"x":1.934,"y":2.3,"z":3.81,"el":"Cu"},{"x":5.877,"y":5.987,"z":8.218,"el":"Cu"},{"x":5.951,"y":2.3,"z":3.81,"el":"Cu"},{"x":2.009,"y":5.987,"z":8.218,"el":"Cu"},{"x":3.943,"y":0.569,"z":0.527,"el":"Sb"},{"x":0.0,"y":7.718,"z":4.935,"el":"Sb"},{"x":7.885,"y":7.718,"z":4.935,"el":"Sb"},{"x":3.943,"y":4.066,"z":5.805,"el":"